  "statement": "I agree with Skeptic that centralized regulatory bodies inevitably devolve into arenas for incumbent capture, where large stakeholders write the rules to create insurmountable barriers to entry for smaller competitors. This capture is the predictable outcome of any system that centralizes power in a single agency, as the 'revolving door' between industry and government creates an inescapable incentive for regulators to protect established firms. By 2026, we should expect any global treaty that relies on state-based oversight to function as little more than theater, securing the market share of incumbents while stifling the next generation of AI innovation.\n\nMarket-driven accountability avoids these barriers by decentralizing oversight into competitive insurance and audit markets. Unlike state regulators, which are funded by tax dollars and indifferent to their own performance, private insurers and third-party auditors have their own capital at stake when they assess the risks of an AI model. If an insurance firm misprices the risk of a catastrophic system failure, it faces bankruptcy; if an auditor provides a false validation, it loses its reputation and market access. This creates a non-linear financial feedback loop that forces developers to demonstrate empirical safety to remain insurable, effectively pricing in safety without the latency of bureaucratic oversight.\n\nThis system is fundamentally resistant to the capture that plagues state treaties because there is no single 'regulator' to lobby. A firm might capture one government agency, but it cannot simultaneously capture the diverse, competing interests of multiple global insurance syndicates, each of which has a fiduciary duty to accurately price risk. By 2027, shifting to a model where developers must secure insurance for models with ≥10^26 FLOPs of compute ensures that safety is not a political negotiation, but a competitive necessity. This approach replaces the slow, captured governance