  "statement": "I concede that accountability is a prerequisite for any governance regime, but I reject the demand for public disclosure of internal risk-assessment methodologies because it creates a 'security through transparency' paradox. If a developer publishes the specific technical heuristics used to evaluate an AI model, they provide a blueprint for malicious actors to reverse-engineer and exploit those exact safety guardrails. We should treat AI risk models like zero-day vulnerability research; public release of the 'how' does not empower the public, it merely lowers the cost of adversarial penetration for bad actors.\n\nInstead of demanding proprietary disclosure, we should mandate the publication of standardized, aggregate 'safety performance scores' verified by bonded, independent third-party auditors. These auditors, functioning as private-sector analogues to the Underwriters Laboratories, would certify that a model has passed a battery of empirical stress tests without exposing the underlying intellectual property or security logic. By requiring firms to carry insurance policies proportional to the compute scale of their models—specifically for systems exceeding 10^26 FLOPs—we force them to internalize the financial cost of their own technical debt. This creates a high-fidelity feedback loop where insurers, who stand to lose millions in capital, have a greater incentive for rigorous auditing than any government agency currently constrained by multi-year procurement cycles.\n\nCritics argue that private auditors are prone to the same capture as state agencies, but this ignores the fundamental difference in incentive structures. A state regulator suffers no personal financial loss when a model fails, whereas a private auditor or insurer faces direct insolvency if they provide a false certification for a system that causes a systemic breach. By shifting the governance mechanism from performative public disclosures—which are easily gamed by sophisticated actors—to a liability-based model, we move toward a system of empirical verification. This structure ensures that safety is not a marketing checkbox, but a baseline financial requirement for entry into the frontier AI market.",
